\newcommand{\arcoth}{\mathop{\rm arcoth}\nolimits}$

The derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is given by

$$
\dfrac{\textrm{d}}{\textrm{d}x}(\arcosh x) = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x^2-1}}.
$$

Therefore, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}\int \frac{\operatorname{ln}⁡\pi }{\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\,\text{d}x=\operatorname{ln}⁡\pi \int \frac{1}{\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\,\text{d}x=\operatorname{ln}⁡\pi \text{\arcosh}x+C.\end{aligned}
$$
